WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:02.760
This is the Ascent Milwaukee.

00:00:03.329 --> 00:00:04.041
Part of it.

00:00:04.041 --> 00:00:05.280
I have to move my camera back.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:11.220
This building’s 284 feet high, 25 stories,
and if you go to the model rooms

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:14.628
it looks like a typical,luxury
apartment building

00:00:14.628 --> 00:00:16.524
with prop wine and art.

00:00:16.836 --> 00:00:21.267
But above the demonstration bed and&nbsp;
view of Milwaukee is a wood ceiling...

00:00:21.715 --> 00:00:22.560
and wood beams...

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:24.360
and wood columns.

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:26.700
They're all structural.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:28.260
That’s because when I visited...

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:30.420
Was that a cool zipper sound?

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:35.000
Ascent Milwaukee was finishing construction.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.460
When I tell someone I'm working on 
the tallest timber building in the world

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.680
they're very surprised to hear that
it's in Milwaukee.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:46.946
How does a building, with wood slabs and beams
like this, actually happen?

00:00:46.946 --> 00:00:51.720
It turns out that changing materials&nbsp;
changes the entire construction process.

00:00:57.699 --> 00:01:01.620
Here’s an elegant demonstration of
a bag of concrete and some rebar.

00:01:02.087 --> 00:01:05.700
A lot of big buildings are built
using steel beams and columns

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:10.443
and PT concrete slab: the PT stands for
Post-tension.

00:01:10.443 --> 00:01:16.200
Basically the steel reinforces the concrete and&nbsp;
tendons are literally pulled on to create tension.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.585
PT Slabs are strong

00:01:18.585 --> 00:01:21.345
relatively easy to build and get

00:01:21.345 --> 00:01:23.145
often pretty affordable

00:01:23.145 --> 00:01:26.280
and, maybe most importantly, 
they’re really familiar

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.863
both to builders and regulators.

00:01:28.863 --> 00:01:31.126
But they’re not very pretty

00:01:31.126 --> 00:01:35.325
and they can require a lot of energy
to make and move around.

00:01:35.325 --> 00:01:37.020
Mass Timber uses, well...

00:01:41.878 --> 00:01:43.200
My name is Ricky McClain.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.420
I'm a senior technical director with Woodworks.

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:49.800
One of the things that the Woodworks&nbsp;
does is tracks the number of projects

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:51.300
that are actually being done with mass timber.

00:01:51.849 --> 00:01:55.980
In 2013, there were say 20-25 projects.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:02:01.140
Whereas now, we're tracking about
1400 projects in the US.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:04.500
So in terms of overall number of projects
done on an annual basis

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:05.880
it's still a small percentage

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.220
but it has been growing significantly.

00:02:08.891 --> 00:02:13.140
The common way that I think of it is like&nbsp;
vertical products and horizontal products.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:14.580
So for columns and beams

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:18.240
it's most often a product called
glue laminated timber or glue lam.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.824
There's adhesive between each layer
pressed together.

00:02:20.824 --> 00:02:23.446
Sides are planed, so a nice smooth surface.

00:02:23.446 --> 00:02:24.720
And so that's the gluelam.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:26.604
Beam, two-bys, and columns are the exact same.

00:02:26.604 --> 00:02:29.127
So the panels, the horizontal panels

00:02:29.127 --> 00:02:33.710
you can kind of think of that&nbsp;
as like replacing a slab system.

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:37.802
Cross laminated timber, CLT, is
one of those panel options

00:02:37.802 --> 00:02:42.900
where you're taking again two bys flat,
a layer of of adhesive goes down

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.440
another layer of two bys flat, but rotated
90 degrees from that first one.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:53.040
So you're creating panels that are
4 to 12 feet wide, 20 to 60 feet long.

00:02:57.584 --> 00:03:00.720
Mass timber is strong,
environmentally in demand

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:04.200
both because wood is a renewable material
and because sometimes

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.765
it earns builders environmental credits

00:03:06.765 --> 00:03:10.560
better — or at least different looking
than concrete

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.880
and arguably better against some&nbsp;
obstacles, like earthquakes and fire.

00:03:15.286 --> 00:03:17.968
It's also hard to get

00:03:17.968 --> 00:03:22.253
and way less familiar to builders
and regulators

00:03:22.253 --> 00:03:26.206
But these materials are more than
lists of pros and cons.

00:03:26.206 --> 00:03:31.770
They can change the process of
design, sourcing and building

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:34.560
for everybody that makes a building
a reality.

00:03:36.223 --> 00:03:39.706
This started as a real estate project,
not as mass timber project.

00:03:39.706 --> 00:03:42.692
The driver was aesthetics.

00:03:42.692 --> 00:03:44.940
At the time, we didn't know much more than that.

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:46.020
It was just a...

00:03:46.426 --> 00:03:50.940
”Isn't it amazing that you can have
a tall structure built out of wood

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:52.620
and the structure is exposed.”

00:03:53.026 --> 00:03:57.480
From that one decision, a lot of things
about making the building change.

00:03:57.480 --> 00:03:59.100
The whole process is different.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:01.080
The insurance is different, the financing.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.000
And then you start getting into construction

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:06.300
and the planning of the building is
completely different

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:09.840
because you're planning so much of&nbsp;
the building ahead of time digitally.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:11.463
Right? Planning and designing.

00:04:11.463 --> 00:04:13.800
The biggest design impact might be the BIM

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:18.705
which takes design and places it at
the top of the construction process.

00:04:18.705 --> 00:04:24.120
A BIM is a “Building Information Model”
a bible of all the stuff in a building.

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:27.540
Imagine a blueprint that’s 3D
and super detailed.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:31.440
We had 60 plus pre-construction meetings.

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:35.197
That experience right away tells&nbsp;
you that "Hey, this is unusual."

00:04:35.197 --> 00:04:36.846
We had to do that because...

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.040
whether the mass timber panels were&nbsp;
being made in Austria or in Canada

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:42.480
or in the United States, it doesn't matter.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:44.553
They're being made at the factory.

00:04:44.553 --> 00:04:47.040
So if something's wrong,
you've got a big problem.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:49.680
It was more intense for us.

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:53.303
So you're basically building what's called
a digital twin to the real building.

00:04:53.303 --> 00:04:57.223
And in the case of timber, because&nbsp;
so much of it is prefabricated

00:04:57.223 --> 00:05:01.380
by the end they had literally modeled
the building down to the last screw.

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:03.480
You always want to get it right&nbsp;
as the structural engineer

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:05.580
or as a design team in general

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:08.280
but normally if there's an issue,
you can cut it

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:11.324
or you can go to the shop or
you can pour more concrete.

00:05:11.324 --> 00:05:13.680
This one, it would've been a
5 month delay to the job

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:15.480
to get another beam shipped over site.

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:19.560
So it really was critical, which was why&nbsp;
we had so many coordination meetings.

00:05:19.865 --> 00:05:21.600
And because mass timber is so new

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:25.800
you have to spend time persuading regulators
to trust it, which means...

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:31.440
There’s actually no sound here.

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:35.284
But this is a mass timber building
being set on fire.

00:05:35.284 --> 00:05:39.237
For years, the USDA’s Forest Products Lab
in Madison, Wisconsin

00:05:39.237 --> 00:05:41.485
has been testing mass timber

00:05:41.485 --> 00:05:46.500
like cross-laminated timber in a building
they constructed just for this purpose.

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:48.060
It performed well.

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:52.200
They also test columns, where the&nbsp;
wood naturally forms a char layer

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:54.480
that protects a column’s stability.

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:57.840
For Ascent, regulators wanted them to test

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:00.960
if the columns could hold up to
three hours of fire.

00:06:01.683 --> 00:06:03.180
So they burned those too.

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:08.640
We did nine columns total of three
of each species that we tested.

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:11.340
We instrumented them with thermocouples.

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:17.263
We lifted the columns out of the furnace
and then scraped all of the char layer off.

00:06:17.263 --> 00:06:23.004
It worked: the columns were fire&nbsp;
resistance rated up to 180 minutes.

00:06:23.004 --> 00:06:24.840
And we looked at different species

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:28.020
because the different species are a little bit
different chemically and might char different.

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:30.120
Char has been studied for hundreds of years.

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:32.760
In general, people know how wood chars.

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:34.920
Concrete and steel can be hurt by fire

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:38.185
but they’re familiar in the United States.

00:06:38.185 --> 00:06:41.580
That’s why, if you look at this video,
you can see that this structure

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:45.124
like many mass timber structures,
is actually a hybrid.

00:06:45.124 --> 00:06:46.860
The parking garage is concrete

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:52.320
and two concrete cores helped get the project&nbsp;
approved more easily than if it were all wood.

00:06:54.823 --> 00:06:57.703
Once design is over, sourcing begins.

00:06:59.618 --> 00:07:04.500
You go from buying concrete at Home Depot
to moving wood across the world.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:09.300
You're moving these absolutely massive pieces&nbsp;
of wood all over the country

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:12.300
And it's trains, planes, automobiles.

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:15.000
You know, you're always trying to&nbsp;
figure out what's the quickest way,

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:18.840
what's the most effective way,
what is the best carbon footprint

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:20.760
you know, all of these items.

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:23.940
Taylor Cabot’s a project manager at Timberlab

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:28.554
which helps design and coordinate
mass timber projects like Ascent.

00:07:28.554 --> 00:07:32.844
Timber manufacturing is still&nbsp;
led by Japan, Germany, and Austria

00:07:32.844 --> 00:07:35.280
and Ascent’s timber comes from Austria,

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:39.606
which means they had to get every&nbsp;
single piece all the way to Milwaukee.

00:07:39.606 --> 00:07:41.726
Imagine you're on the Oregon Trail

00:07:41.726 --> 00:07:46.740
and you have glass panes for your window
and you have it in your wagon

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:48.480
with like hay around it.

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:52.980
And it's like if you break that glass pane,&nbsp;
like you are not going to get another one.

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:56.580
We are moving things across the country.

00:07:56.580 --> 00:08:01.807
It can be replaced, but you're&nbsp;
talking weeks. Not days.

00:08:01.807 --> 00:08:05.000
These materials then affect the engineering.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:09.000
It's not like a steel or a concrete building&nbsp;
where I, you know, the architect doesn't care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:11.760
what what kind of steel I pick or what&nbsp;
kind of concrete as long as it works.

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:14.220
The owner really likes this
white Austrian spruce.

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:17.160
So some of the columns at the base,&nbsp;
you know, to minimize the overall size

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:20.220
are a higher grade or they're&nbsp;
a little stronger, stiffer.

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:21.780
But that's also more expensive.

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:24.660
So as you go up, you sort of use
the lower grades

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:26.280
as it becomes more cost effective.

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:29.640
Traditional construction also
isn’t affected by stuff like

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:31.980
a boat getting stuck in the Suez Canal

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:34.200
Our materials didn't go through
the Suez Canal

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:39.600
but 2 million containers were then
held up in transit as well because

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:43.260
they were either stuck literally in the&nbsp;
canal, or waiting to get in the canal.

00:08:43.727 --> 00:08:46.440
Finding a container was like gold.

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:49.500
I would be driving in my city

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:55.200
and seeing a container sitting in a yard,
and I was like — should we go grab it?

00:09:00.422 --> 00:09:02.342
All those design and sourcing changes

00:09:02.342 --> 00:09:04.860
mean building with mass timber
is pretty different.

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:10.740
Our construction workers here, they would
go home at the end of the day

00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:12.420
and their wives would say

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:14.580
"Huh, doesn't even look like
you're working today."

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:15.240
"You're so clean."

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:18.120
And they love that because with
the timber you're not working with

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:20.760
all this silica dust from concrete
and everything like that.

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:21.780
You're just working with with wood

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:25.140
and you're really not cutting much,
if at all.

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:26.820
So you're going home clean.

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:31.200
Stuff like welding just doesn’t happen
in a wood building

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:33.240
because it’d be a fire hazard.

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:37.665
So, screwdrivers become way more important.

00:09:37.665 --> 00:09:40.784
We would have upwards of 64 16 inch screws.

00:09:40.784 --> 00:09:44.327
We were using just cordless
battery operated tools.

00:09:44.327 --> 00:09:49.305
We'd only get maybe five or six of those
16 inch screws per battery.

00:09:49.305 --> 00:09:54.120
We had a pretty substantial battery changing&nbsp;
and charging operation on the working deck

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:55.440
as we're installing these screws.

00:09:55.440 --> 00:10:00.420
So we would have to field install
roughly 7000 screws per floor.

00:10:02.542 --> 00:10:04.522
This is a glue lam column.

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:09.480
Below our topping we have our CLT plank
and we have some beams

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:11.040
that run into the column.

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:17.220
So one column may have as much as 64
just CLT to column screws.

00:10:18.763 --> 00:10:20.765
Because of this, a lot of things change.

00:10:20.765 --> 00:10:23.580
While it might take 10 days to complete
a floor with concrete

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:26.426
it could take only 5, using mass timber.

00:10:26.426 --> 00:10:29.730
There's less skilled labor available.

00:10:29.730 --> 00:10:35.767
There's this inherent push to to fully&nbsp;
detail out a building beforehand.

00:10:35.767 --> 00:10:41.340
And so it really helps reduce labor force
on site or reduce waste of materials

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:43.167
that come out to site as well.

00:10:43.167 --> 00:10:48.523
And it's this kind of huge shift in
the industry where we're saying,

00:10:48.523 --> 00:10:51.104
we don't need 100 guys to build a building.

00:10:51.104 --> 00:10:52.905
You know, we might need 30.

00:10:53.742 --> 00:10:58.205
Or we don't need to bring a bunch&nbsp;
of materials to site and cut them

00:10:58.205 --> 00:11:00.600
and create a bunch of waste
and send the waste away.

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:03.960
We can do all that work in factories
and then send it out

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:09.846
and have it kind of erect in Lincoln log
or a Lego set sort of form sort of way.

00:11:09.846 --> 00:11:14.760
And that goes back to the original&nbsp;
question: how does a wood skyscraper happen?

00:11:16.423 --> 00:11:20.863
They’re the product of a total rethinking&nbsp;
of the process of construction.

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:29.100
One of my favorite just anecdotes was that

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:35.640
when we framed the first deck of timber
and we go up there, and I'm like

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:37.260
"What am I sensing?"

00:11:37.260 --> 00:11:41.580
And I laid down on the deck because
it doesn't have the concrete top yet.

00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:42.683
Right? It's just raw wood.

00:11:42.683 --> 00:11:44.520
And I lay down and they look at me
and they're like

00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:47.340
"What is wrong with you and
what are you doing?"

00:11:47.848 --> 00:11:50.682
And I said, "Guys, smell it."

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:53.363
"It smells like popsicle sticks."

00:11:53.363 --> 00:11:55.800
And they're like, "You're insane!"

00:11:55.800 --> 00:11:56.880
And they get down and they're like

00:11:56.880 --> 00:11:58.500
"Oh, my God, it smells like popsicle sticks."

